  "name": "Question 14",
  "problem": "Expressed in **ascending order** in degrees, the exact solutions to the equation $\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x=\\frac{\\sqrt{12}}{3},$ for $-{180}^{\u2218}\\le x<{180}^{\u2218},$  are\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}${}^{\u2218},\\,x=$\n{{field-2}}${}^{\u2218}.$",
  "worked_solution": "First, we simplify the given equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{12}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}\u2061x & =\\frac{\\sqrt{4\\cdot 3}}{3} \\\\ \\operatorname{csc}\u2061x & =\\frac{2\\sqrt{3}}{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that $\\operatorname{csc}\u2061x=\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x}.$ Therefore, the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x=\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}}.\n$$\n\nThen, we find the principal value:\n\n$$\nx=\\operatorname{arcsin}\u2061(\\frac{3}{2\\sqrt{3}})={60}^{\u2218}\n$$\n\nSince ${60}^{\u2218}$ lies inside the required domain, our first solution is ${x}_{1}={60}^{\u2218}.$\n\nOur first solution lies in the first quadrant and has a reference angle of ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{\u2218}.$ Since $\\operatorname{sin}\u2061x>0,$ the second solution must lie in the fouth quadrant, and it also has a reference angle ${\\theta }_{R}={60}^{\u2218}.$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/13978818/assets/4cb8f27f6c108091d233ec0f2a2dbb3439c7df93747917e6a50db770642e76f8.png)\n\nSince the second solution lies in the $2$nd quadrant, we can find it using the reference angle as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{2} & ={180}^{\u2218}-{\\theta }_{R} \\\\  & ={180}^{\u2218}-{60}^{\u2218} \\\\  & ={120}^{\u2218}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the solutions to the equation, in ascending order, are \n\n$$\nx={60}^{\u2218},\\,x={120}^{\u2218}.\n$$",
